  {
   "type": "text",
   "title": "How IP reputation blocking works",
   "id": 8,
   "gridPos": {
    "h": 6,
    "w": 16,
    "x": 8,
    "y": 19
   },
   "options": {
    "mode": "markdown",
    "code": {
     "language": "plaintext",
     "showLineNumbers": false,
     "showMiniMap": false
    },
    "content": "**Choose feeds:** click a feed in *Feed Catalogue* to add it, or in *Enabled Feeds* to remove it (`all` in the remove box clears every one, and empties the router list). Feeds are merged smallest first into one address list, `reputation-blocked` by default; an address two feeds share is credited to the smaller one.\n\n**It blocks nothing until the router references the list** -- by hand, on purpose, exactly like the IDS block list:\n`/ip firewall raw add chain=prerouting action=drop src-address-list=reputation-blocked`  \n`/ip firewall raw add chain=prerouting action=drop dst-address-list=reputation-blocked`  \nThe first drops what listed hosts send in, the second stops anything inside reaching them (the botnet C2 feeds are about that one). The tile at the top reads NO FIREWALL RULE until one exists.\n\n**Never blocked, whatever a feed says:** private, reserved, multicast and CGNAT space, `suricata.home_net`, `whitelist.*`, the router and `ip_reputation.never`. *Dropped* in the table is how many entries that removed -- FireHOL level1 really does list 10.0.0.0/8. Entries expire router-side after `ip_reputation.entry_timeout` unless refreshed, so the list fails open if this stack stops.\n\n**Anyone who can view this dashboard can change the feeds** -- the call runs as the datasource, not the viewer. Every change is in the audit table at the bottom."
   }
  },
